<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-alanine biosynthesis III" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY0-1021" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Alanine is an essential component of protein (as |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE|) and peptidoglycan (as a roughly 3:1 mix of |FRAME: D-ALANINE| and |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE|). Only about a tenth of total alanine synthesized is incorporated into peptidoglycan. At least three pathways (|FRAME: ALANINE-VALINESYN-PWY|, |FRAME: ALANINE-SYN2-PWY|, and |FRAME: PWY0-1021|) are known for production of |FRAME: L-ALPHA-ALANINE|. This pathway is carried out in a single step by |FRAME: EC-2.8.1.7|, in a reaction that is critical for generation of iron-sulfur clusters, thiouridine groups in tRNAs, and a number of other sulfur-and selenium-containing proteins. Although this pathway does generate alanine, a major role in generating the cellular supply of alanine has not been demonstrated.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="O0-3" />
    <Author name="ingraham" />
    <Author name="shearer" />
  </Authors>
